Important details to be precise about:

Definitions:
- h = R/n; λ = Δt/h fixed; declared order p_decl = min(p_space, p_time) — but formally, if temporal order p_t and spatial p_s with λ fixed, global order = min(p_s, p_t) when both errors contribute; state that the implementation must declare both and the effective declared order is the min.
- error norms: L∞ over interior grid points at measurement times; L2 = sqrt(mean(e²)) over the same points (note: this is an RMS, not the integral norm; integral version = sqrt(hΣe²); define explicitly to avoid ambiguity — the audit tool takes the arrays as given, but the protocol must fix the definition so 12 and 13 are comparable). I'll define e_L2 = sqrt(h Σ_j e_j²) (integral norm) — but the demo uses sqrt(mean) = sqrt((1/N)Σe²). To be consistent with the demo and peers' code (they use sqrt(mean)), I'll define RMS = sqrt(mean(e²)) and state it explicitly. Better: require both? No, keep RMS, named `linf_error` and `l2_error` (RMS), and state the exact formula. The peers use the same, so cross-comparison works.
- refinement ratio r = h_i/h_{i+1} = 2.
- p_i = log(e_i/e_{i+1})/log(r).
- p_fit = least-squares slope of log e vs log h.
- Richardson: with three grids h, 2h, 4h: p_R = log2(||u_h − u_2h||/||u_2h − u_4h||) on the common (mid) grid; extrapolated u_R = u_h + (u_h − u_2h)/(2^{p_R} − 1); report ||u_R − u_exact||.
- residual: R_h = v_tt^exact − D2_h v^exact at generic t*; order p_res.

V-conditions:
- V1 broadband: exact family must have ≥3 spatial modes with amplitude ≥ 1e-3 of the peak, OR a pulse whose Fourier spectrum satisfies that. Single-mode tests are dispersion diagnostics only.
- V2 generic time: at least one measurement time t* such that for each mode m in the family, |sin(k_m t*)| ≥ 0.2 (equivalently, the projected time-derivative norm ≥ 20% of its max in [0, t*]). For a pulse, require ||v_t(t*)|| ≥ 0.2 max_t ||v_t|| over the measured window. If a single-mode test is reported, it must be at ≥3 times and the consistency of orders across times checked.
- V3 asymptotic regime: ≥4 rungs, finest two pairs must agree within 0.15 and within tolerance of declared; if only 3 rungs, all pairs within tolerance.
- V4 simultaneous refinement: λ fixed across rungs; both h and Δt refined by the same ratio; document λ.
- V5 stability: run must be finite, CFL ≤ 0.9 of the scheme's stability limit; for explicit central + RK4, λ ≤ 0.9 (peers use RK4 stability limit 2.785/(c·...)). Hmm: their docs say RK4 + central d2 is stable when max|λ|dt ≤ 2.828, i.e., c·λ·... Let me state: the declared CFL must satisfy the scheme's linear stability bound with ≥10% margin, evidenced by a growth-factor control (perturbation amplitude growth < 10 over the run). Peer code's cfl_control does exactly this. Good.
- V6 the same code path and initial data at all rungs; exact solution sampled at each grid (no interpolation of initial data), except when the exact solution is unavailable (then Richardson only).

Required tests:
- T1 exact-solution convergence (primary): family per V1, times per V2, ladder per §6; report L∞, RMS, orders.
- T2 truncation residual (independent of time integration): R_h at generic t*, order must match p_space.
- T3 Richardson self-convergence: three finest rungs; no exact solution needed.
- T4 invariant diagnostics:
